\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
To interpret this, we need to lay out the evidence for what these figures mean. Let us begin with the mother lion. To what, in the Torah, would a lion refer? Especially with these particular words for lions, having cubs who are also lions, and the later reference to a scepter? Let's lay out the evidence thoroughly as a foundation for what comes next.\
Then, who are the likely candidates for each of the two cubs? Let's lay out evidence for and against each candidate.
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Opus:\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Looking at the Torah foundations for this imagery:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 The Lion imagery:
\b0 \cf2 \
The most direct connection is Genesis 49:9: 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1512 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1497 \u1461 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1468 \u1491 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  - "Judah is a lion's cub (
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1512 
\f1 \cf2 )." Ezekiel uses this exact term 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1512 
\f1 \cf2  for the cubs. The Genesis passage continues: 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1496 \u1462 \u1468 \u1512 \u1462 \u1507 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 \u1514 \u1464 
\f1 \cf2  - "from the prey, my son, you have gone up." Note the shared vocabulary of 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1496 \u1462 \u1512 \u1462 \u1507 
\f1 \cf2  (prey/tearing).\
Genesis 49:10 provides the scepter connection: 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 \u1470 \u1497 \u1464 \u1505 \u1493 \u1468 \u1512 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1513 \u1461 \u1473 \u1489 \u1462 \u1496 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1497 \u1492 \u1493 \u1468 \u1491 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  - "The scepter (
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1513 \u1461 \u1473 \u1489 \u1462 \u1496 
\f1 \cf2 ) shall not depart from Judah." Ezekiel 19:11 uses both 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1496 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f1 \cf2  (rods/branches) and 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1489 \u1456 \u1496 \u1461 \u1497 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1502 \u1465 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  (scepters of rulers), while verse 14 laments there's no longer a 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1513 \u1461 \u1473 \u1489 \u1462 \u1496 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1502 \u1456 \u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1465 \u1500 
\f1 \cf2  (scepter to rule).\
The term 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1464 \u1468 \u1488 
\f1 \cf2  for lioness appears in Genesis 49:9 as well (
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1488 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1497 \u1461 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1499 \u1456 \u1500 \u1464 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1488 
\f1 \cf2 ), and Numbers 23:24 uses it for Israel: 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1492 \u1462 \u1503 \u1470 \u1506 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1500 \u1464 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1488 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1511 \u1493 \u1468 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  - "Behold, a people rises like a lioness."\
The term 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1508 \u1460 \u1497 \u1512 
\f1 \cf2  (young lion) that both cubs become appears in Judges 14:5 for the lion Samson kills, suggesting full-grown strength, not just youth.\

\b \cf2 The mother lion:
\b0 \cf2  Given the Judah-lion connection and the royal/scepter context, the mother is almost certainly the Davidic dynasty or Judah itself as the royal tribe. The feminine personification matches how cities and nations are portrayed throughout Ezekiel.\

\b \cf2 First cub (verses 3-4):
\b0 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Taken to Egypt with hooks (
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1495 \u1463 \u1495 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1 )\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The nations "heard" about him\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 Evidence for Jehoahaz:
\b0 \cf2  2 Kings 23:31-34 records Jehoahaz reigning three months before Pharaoh Neco imprisoned him and took him to Egypt where he died. The brief reign and Egyptian captivity match perfectly.\

\b \cf2 Against Jehoahaz:
\b0 \cf2  He wasn't particularly noted for violence or "devouring men" in the historical record.\

\b \cf2 Second cub (verses 5-9):
\b0 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
More elaborate description of his violence\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
"Knew his widows" and destroyed cities\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Taken to Babylon (not Egypt)\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Put in a cage (
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1505 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1490 \u1463 \u1512 
\f1 )\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 Evidence for Jehoiachin:
\b0 \cf2  2 Kings 24:8-15 shows him taken to Babylon after three months. Ezekiel dates visions by his exile (1:2), suggesting his significance.\

\b \cf2 Against Jehoiachin:
\b0 \cf2  Like Jehoahaz, his brief reign doesn't match the extensive violence described. The text suggests someone who had time to establish systematic oppression.\

\b \cf2 Evidence for Zedekiah:
\b0 \cf2  Last king of Judah, reigned eleven years, giving time for the systematic violence described. His rebellion against Babylon (2 Kings 24:20) could be the "roaring" that provoked capture.\

\b \cf2 Against Zedekiah:
\b0 \cf2  2 Kings 25:7 says his sons were killed before him and his eyes put out - different from being caged. Also, verse 5 suggests the second cub was raised after the first was taken, but Zedekiah was Jehoahaz's brother, not a later generation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> The historical identification matters less than the pattern Ezekiel establishes - royal power learning violence rather than justice, with each generation reproducing and intensifying the pattern until the entire system consumes itself. </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The ambiguity might be intentional - the cubs represent a pattern of Davidic kings who abandoned their Genesis 49 calling to establish justice and became predators instead.
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
