\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You're absolutely right - I overlooked Jehoiakim, who is actually the strongest candidate for the second cub.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 The evidence for Jehoiakim is compelling:
\b0 \cf2 \
The sequential logic of verse 5 is crucial: 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1514 \u1461 \u1468 \u1512 \u1462 \u1488 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1504 \u1493 \u1465 \u1495 \u1458 \u1500 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1489 \u1456 \u1491 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1511 \u1456 \u1493 \u1464 \u1514 \u1464 \u1492 \u1468 
\f1 \cf2  - "When she saw that she waited in vain, that her hope was lost." This describes exactly the situation after Egypt installed Eliakim/Jehoiakim as Jehoahaz's replacement. The mother "took another of her cubs" - not birthed a new one but elevated an existing son to kingship.\
The violence description matches precisely. 2 Kings 24:4's "filled Jerusalem with innocent blood" (
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1511 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1 \cf2 ) connects directly to Ezekiel's 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1491 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1499 \u1464 \u1500 
\f1 \cf2  - "devoured humans." The 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1497 \u1461 \u1468 \u1491 \u1463 \u1506 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1500 \u1456 \u1502 \u1456 \u1504 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1497 \u1493 
\f1 \cf2  could reference both sexual violence and the widows created by his bloodshed, then exploited through crushing taxation (2 Kings 23:35).\
The "destroying cities" (
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1506 \u1464 \u1512 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1492 \u1462 \u1495 \u1457 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 \u1489 
\f1 \cf2 ) likely refers to the devastating raids by multiple nations that 2 Kings 24:2 explicitly states were YHWH's response to Jehoiakim's bloodshed - Chaldeans, Arameans, Moabites, and Ammonites all ravaging Judah during his reign.\

\b \cf2 The connections to chapters 17-18:
\b0 \cf2 \
Chapter 17's vine that turns from its planter to seek another source connects to chapter 19's vine that produces fire instead of fruit. Both involve Davidic rulers (Zedekiah there, the dynasty here) who broke faith with their divinely-appointed role.\
