\b \cf2 They're stuck in a victim narrative.
\b0 \cf2  Chapter 18's "fathers ate sour grapes" shows they've constructed an elaborate theodicy where they're innocent sufferers of ancestral sins. Chapter 12's "the vision is for distant times" reveals they deflect any immediate application. They've built interpretive frameworks that always make them the wronged party.\

\b \cf2 They maintain delusional confidence.
\b0 \cf2  In Ezekiel 11:3, those still in Jerusalem say "this city is the pot, and we are the meat" - believing they're protected and valuable. Even after Jehoiachin's exile, they think they're the true remnant (11:15): "they have gone far from the LORD; to us this land is given for a possession."\
Jeremiah's letter (Jeremiah 29) reveals the exiles were listening to prophets promising quick return - Shemaiah, Ahab, Zedekiah (different from the king) all feeding fantasies of imminent restoration. They're constructing alternative realities rather than facing what happened.\

\b \cf2 The riddle-forcing strategy targets this precisely:
\b0 \cf2 \
By making them solve "which cub is which king," Ezekiel forces them to rehearse the actual history - Jehoahaz taken to Egypt, Jehoiakim filling Jerusalem with blood, the nations responding to violence. They can't hide behind "we were attacked unprovoked." The neutral-to-positive portrayal of the nations (they "heard" and responded appropriately to predation) dismantles the victim narrative.\
The 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1491 \u1464 \u1502 \u1456 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1 \cf2  ambiguity is brilliant. If they read it as "in blood," they must acknowledge the dynasty was planted in violence from the beginning - undercutting claims of legitimate rule. If they read it as "in your likeness/vigor," they must recognize the pattern reproduction - each generation choosing the same violence. Either reading forces recognition of agency and continuity.\

\b \cf2 Evidence from contemporary sources:
\b0 \cf2 \
Lamentations (likely contemporary) shows the same mental state - cycling between self-pity, blaming God, and brief moments of recognizing sin. They need shocking rhetorical strategies to break through the defensive narratives.\
Jeremiah 44:16-19 shows those who fled to Egypt openly declaring they'll continue worshiping the Queen of Heaven, blaming their troubles on stopping her worship rather than idolatry itself. They've inverted cause and effect completely.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> The audience has constructed elaborate deflection mechanisms - theological, historical, and psychological. Normal prophetic speech bounces off these defenses. The perverted lament forces them to participate in deconstructing their own delusions by making them solve the riddles. </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

