That bit about no scepter to rule is too close too close to the explicit claim in Genesis 49 that 'the scepter shall not depart' to be coincidence. \
Similarly, the covenant with David is clear that the throne will be established forever. Iniquity on the part of Davidic kings is explicitly discussed, but the result is discipline: "When he commits iniquity, I will discipline him with the rod of men, with the stripes of the sons of men, but my steadfast love will not depart from him, as I took it from Saul, whom I put away from before you." Indeed, the agent of that discipline being foreign nations is quite clear in the prophets, though it goes against what the people believed. Those foreign nations acting to handle the Davidic kings as one might handle a problem lion is present in Ezekiel 19 as well. \
Still, even if all that happens is that one king is captured by Babylon and another destroys himself and his country from within, and both of them lose all rule in the process, that very much goes against the standard thought of the day. Moreover, the resulting destruction leaves no room for a human hope of restoration. Verse 5 raises the question of what the nation hopes in only to prepare for the possibility that those hopes are already lost, not just will be lost. How much more after the fire of the final destruction of Jerusalem and the dynasty, and the replanting that is the last and most thorough exile.\
That arrogance and seeking to be a peer of foreign nations you were speaking of may have infected Ezekiel's audience as well. That may be reflected in the towering of the vine, and being seen in its height. Verse 12 then answers that arrogance.\
Now, we've gone at least some distance towards understanding the nature of the rhetoric in the chapter. But we ought not forget the prophetic nature of this rhetoric. That is to say, this is a message from God, through his prophet. Some of the purposes we have discussed - but there are reasons for those purposes in the character of God and the history of his dealings with his people. Some of the evidence for that will be in this chapter, some in previous parts of the Bible. Until we understand that, we are only scratching the surface of this lament.
