This is a good start. Looking at the places that puzzle us is necessary work to understand what we can from the text.\
On one level, of course, the roaring is natural to the lion metaphor. But the lion metaphor is at least somewhat stretched in verse 7. Real lions do not usually lay waste to cities. Nor do they do so usually in Scripture. More typical and expected would be following Numbers 23:24 where the people of Israel is compared to a lion which does not rest until it has eaten prey. \
Similarly, roaring in the Bible is more associated with the predatory or aggressive side of lions, as in Judges 14:5 where Sampson faces a real roaring lion, or in Psalm 22 where the roaring lions represent human aggression, possibly verbal and deceitful, or in Ezekiel 22:15 where roaring lions announcing they have already taken prey are used to describe a conspiracy of prophets, \
However, we have two possible clues to the meaning of the roaring here. First, Proverbs 28:15 says "Like a roaring lion or a charging bear is a wicked ruler over a poor people." It is specifically his own land and the fullness of it, that is, the whole populace, that is appalled at the roaring of teh king. His oppression of the poor, then, may be compared to both going after whoever is weaker to oppress them, and making the rest feel afraid.\
Second, and more likely, is Jeremiah 2:15, which says of Egypt attacking and humbling Israel "The lions have roared against him;\'a0they have roared loudly. They have made his land a waste; his cities are in ruins, without inhabitant." While not all the words in Hebrew are exactly the same, this is a close match to verse 7. Moreover, Ezekiel has somewhat of a habit of drawing on Jeremiah. Here, as elsewhere, lions roaring are associated with their predatory nature and taking prey. The question Jeremiah asks is why Israel has become prey. The answer is their having forsaken the LORD. But of most use to us is the more detailed metaphor, which may use roaring as a metaphor for the fear which depopulates cities ahead of the threat of killing and the like. This may fit with the Daniel 5:19 evidence you raise. That roaring may be the fear of the people - but here, fear of their own king.\
The cage terminology may reflect actual practices with both lions and human prisoners in war. Pit traps were a standard way of initiating a capture on a dangerous animal. Hooks through flesh were a standard Assyrian way of making sure prisoners of war were compliant while being transported into exile or the like, so perhaps this is why the LORD uses hooks as a metaphor for involuntarily moving the king of Assyria out of the land of Judah in 2 Kings 28. \
A cage, however, is how one might move a dangerous animal like a lion. This is where the discussion of roaring comes in - the roaring is the proximate cause of the attack by the nations. The land and its people are appalled at the roaring, and the nations come because they hear this dangerous lion roaring. That is, the lion is described, as in the other verses, as a threat to his own people, a threat the other nations put down. Verse 9 makes this even more explicit with a purpose clause for the movement in cages - ending the roaring (literally his voice) against the mountains of Israel. The cage terminology supports this - this is a dangerous animal requiring a cage for safe transport. \
A slightly different form of the word is used in Ezekiel 12:13 to describe a different king, Zedekiah, being taken to Babylon, though it is not at all clear that this is a physical description of the means used , as that passage speaks of the LORD acting as a hunter to move Zedekiah involuntarily. Ezekiel 17:21 speaks of this capture of Zedekiah in much the same way.\
The walking among lions is an element of verses 5 and 6 which is not, unlike the rest, repeated from verse 3. We see this verb in this form in Genesis 13:17, in which Abraham is told to walk the land of Canaan. The reason given is that the LORD will give this land to Abraham. So taking or showing possession is in view. We mentioned earlier showing his status among other kings as a possibility for interpreting this verse, presumably because if there are other lions, who else would they be? But also, both interpretations of sexual relations with widows may have an element of taking possession. Taking the previous king's wives and concubines is a clear act of demonstrating one's possession of the kingship, and is described as such in the David narrative. Similarly, sleeping with the widows of those murdered may be as much about demonstrating power as more sexual motives.\
Being seen in its height in verse 11 is, on the surface, a way of expressing great height. A tall plant is visible in a way a short one is not, and from a greater distance. Slightly more idiomatically, this is a claim to some sort of objective, visible greatness of the vine and what it represents, Judah or the monarchy as a whole. Moreover, it is not just the height which can be seen - the many branches can also be seen. The fruitfulness, the fecundity, of the vine is a testimony to the nation itself and to the surrounding nations. It can be seen. The cause of this fruitfulness and branch production was given in verse 10 - her planting beside many waters. This may also be a reference to Ezekiel 17, though the vine there is a specific king. Still, who planted the vine is clear enough even apart from Ezekiel 17.\
The final "from it" in verse 14 is somewhat technical. There are many ways to read a construct. Consider the following option: "from the staff which is constituted by her shoots". That staff would be, as in verse 12, a ruler's staff, connecting the staff metaphor for the authority of particular rulers with the biological metaphor of the shoots connected to the larger vine. The misuse of ruling authority would then be the source of the fire, but the connection of the shoot to the vine causes that misuse to be disaster for the whole nation.\
The timing puzzle is, I think, best solved with the suggestions you made earlier about reasons for holding a funeral for someone who is still alive.\
With this evidence in mind, how might these initially puzzling elements connect to the rest of what Ezekiel is saying, both the immediately neighboring rhetorical moves and the rhetoric as a whole?
